  "chosen_value": 96,
  "why": "Monotone on every task with diminishing returns, and 96 is where the budget stops being the binding constraint: exhaustions fall to 8.6 / 1.0 / 0.8 per hundred attempts and chemical validity reaches 99.2 / 99.1 / 99.5, so any remaining loss is chemistry rather than budget. Beyond this point a larger budget has almost nothing left to recover.",
  "cost": "wall clock only: motif 204 -> 384 s per shard, superstructure 336 -> 392, decoration 398 -> 449. Exhausting the budget ends a trajectory, so the knob trades sampling effort for attempts that finish and cannot change what is chemically admissible.",
  "caveat": "Secondary metrics improve rather than narrow: motif uniqueness 78.22 -> 89.86 and quality 14.47 -> 21.03, diversity 0.60 -> 0.61. The larger budget lets harder trajectories finish rather than biasing toward easy ones."
}